# Point-in-time registry evidence clock and neighborhood expert

## Core idea

Recover each sanitized trial’s NCT identity using only registry snapshots available before its prediction origin. Build a compact external expert from results-submission/QC state, completion and enrollment evolution, pre-origin publications, and as-of success histories of related sponsors, conditions, interventions, and facilities. Blend it with the frozen `run_0007` incumbent using weights selected exclusively on training-period forward gates.

This is ranked first because it changes the available information, rather than polishing the incumbent’s representation. Its credible ceiling is approximately 0.85–0.93 if linkage is accurate and public/pending evidence is sufficiently common; low evidence coverage will reduce the gain sharply.

## Implementation steps

1. Bank the incumbent before experimentation.

   - Confirm that `run_0007` is the session’s registered final fallback; do not edit evaluator files or private selection metadata directly.
   - Copy its validation/test vectors into the shared cache, record their SHA-256 hashes, and verify the validation vector rescoring at 0.7246637092.
   - Read `features_history.md` and `table_information.md`. Append this branch as `PROPOSED`.
   - Instrument one cache-first incumbent build to persist aligned forward-OOF, validation, and test vectors for every invariant channel. Never rebuild those channels afterward.

2. Use a staged archive plan with a hard time gate.

   The official AACT site provides permanent monthly archives from 2018 onward, irregular 2017 archives, and roughly 0.6–1.1 GB compressed snapshots for the relevant years: [2017 archives](https://aact.ctti-clinicaltrials.org/downloads/snapshots?type=pgdump&year=2017), [2018](https://aact.ctti-clinicaltrials.org/downloads/snapshots?type=pgdump&year=2018), [2019](https://aact.ctti-clinicaltrials.org/downloads/snapshots?type=pgdump&year=2019), [2020](https://aact.ctti-clinicaltrials.org/downloads/snapshots?type=pgdump&year=2020).

   - Start with snapshots strictly preceding these origins:
     - 2017-06-13 → July 2017 replay;
     - 2017-12-17 → 2018 official train;
     - 2018-06-01 → July 2018 replay;
     - 2018-12-01 → 2019 official train;
     - 2019-12-01 → validation at 2020-01-01;
     - 2020-12-01 → test at 2021-01-01.
   - Never use a same-day archive for a midnight origin.
   - Download one snapshot first and measure bandwidth/extraction time. If the six-snapshot projection exceeds 60 minutes, retain only the four annual snapshots and omit replay gating.
   - Project only required tables and columns into Parquet rather than restoring every table into PostgreSQL.
   - Record URL, archive date, SHA-256, parser version, and maximum usable timestamp in `changes.log`.

3. Resolve identities conservatively.

   - Normalize titles using Unicode normalization, case folding, punctuation/whitespace folding, and removal of obvious boilerplate suffixes.
   - Match exact `official_title` or `brief_title` plus compatible start date first.
   - Block unresolved candidates by start date ±31 days, phase, sponsor/source, and enrollment. Use a one-to-one assignment.
   - Admit fuzzy matches only when:
     - similarity/posterior is at least 0.995;
     - the margin over the runner-up is at least 0.05;
     - at least two unused audit fields agree.
   - The candidate NCT record must come from a snapshot dated before that row’s origin. A later snapshot may not be used merely to recover an earlier identity.
   - Audit at least 100 random exact/fuzzy matches against unused fields. Require estimated precision ≥99% and high-confidence linkage ≥80% overall and ≥90% for validation/test. Unmatched rows receive explicit missing indicators and route to the incumbent.
   - Abort the external blend if precision fails, even if raw coverage is high.

4. Produce a minimal registry-clock score first.

   Build these features from the latest safe snapshot:

   - overall status and status duration;
   - days since verification, update, primary completion, and completion;
   - actual versus anticipated completion/enrollment;
   - actual-to-planned enrollment ratio;
   - primary-completion slippage and enrollment revisions where two safe snapshots exist;
   - facility count, country count, recruiting-site share, and year-over-year site change;
   - results-first-submitted, QC-qualified, first-posted, disposition, extension, and pending-results states;
   - counts and recencies of submissions, QC returns, cancellations, and outcome-definition changes;
   - primary-outcome title/time-frame changes;
   - missingness and linkage-confidence features.

   Fit a small LightGBM expert immediately and obtain its first internal score before adding literature or history reconstruction.

5. Add as-of relational neighborhood evidence cheaply.

   From each safe snapshot, reproduce the official primary-analysis validity filters for non-target trials whose results were already public by that snapshot. Exclude the target trial itself.

   Compute empirical-Bayes success/count/reporting-lag aggregates over:

   - lead sponsor and sponsor class;
   - condition and condition×phase;
   - intervention and intervention×condition;
   - facility, country, and sponsor×condition;
   - one-hop and two-hop trial neighborhoods.

   Include neighbor counts, significant rates, minimum/median recency, reporting lag, degree, and uncertainty. Apply shrinkage toward the global snapshot rate and add within-origin ranks/z-scores.

6. Add registry-version histories only after the minimal gate passes.

   The maintained [`cthist`](https://github.com/bgcarlisle/cthist) package exposes registry version dates, status, completion/enrollment state, outcome measures, references, and results-posted flags.

   - Query histories only for confidently linked recent training/replay/validation/test trials.
   - Use versions with `version_date < origin.date`; never expose the number or existence of later versions.
   - Derive 30/90/365/730-day update counts, status transitions, completion drift, enrollment revisions, site churn, primary-outcome edits, and time in each status.
   - Cache every version payload and derived row by NCT ID, origin, source version, and parser hash.

7. Add a pre-origin literature evidence expert.

   - Begin with RESULT references present in the safe AACT/registry version, then optionally query Europe PMC by the linked NCT accession and title.
   - Require a conservative public-availability date before the origin. Exclude documents with ambiguous dates that could cross the cutoff.
   - Supply the registered primary endpoint, arms, population, sample size, and complete eligible abstract to the existing hosted extraction pipeline. Omit NCT ID from the model prompt.
   - Extract constrained fields rather than a free-form label guess:
     - article type;
     - registered-primary-endpoint match;
     - population/arm match;
     - final versus interim result;
     - direction;
     - explicit primary p-value, if stated;
     - confidence and insufficient-evidence markers.
   - Protocols, secondary-only analyses, subgroups, and interim reports remain distinct categories.
   - Cache by model, prompt version, origin, PMID/version, and content hash.
   - Fit an elastic-net logistic expert over these fields; do not hard-code a positive/negative override.

8. Use honest recent-origin gates.

   - Generate July replay labels with the exact supplied label query. Exclude validation/test NCT IDs and group all repeated rows by NCT ID.
   - Gate A: train on July 2017 replay rows whose windows end by July 2018, evaluate July 2018.
   - Gate B: train on 2018 official rows, evaluate 2019 official rows.
   - If only annual archives are available, use the 2019 origin as the sealed gate and treat the reduced evidence as higher uncertainty.
   - Fit a fold-specific domain discriminator and clip density-ratio weights toward the next-origin
